---
import "../../styles/global.css";
import { siteConfig } from "../../config";

const pageTitle = "Building FMAP: an interactive astro-ph paper map with embeddings, UMAP, and arXiv metadata";
const pageDescription =
  "A technical write-up of FMAP: astro-ph ingestion from arXiv, TF-IDF + LinearSVC classification, sentence embeddings, UMAP projection, evaluation plots, and an embedded interactive atlas.";
---

<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href="/favicon.svg" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width" />
    <meta name="generator" content={Astro.generator} />
    <meta name="description" content={pageDescription} />
    <title>{pageTitle} | {siteConfig.name}</title>
  </head>
  <body class="blog-page">
    <main class="px-6 py-12 sm:px-10 md:px-14 md:py-16 lg:px-16 xl:px-20">
      <article class="blog-shell mx-auto max-w-7xl">
        <a href="/#blog" class="text-sm font-medium text-gray-500 hover:text-gray-900">← Back to portfolio</a>

        <header class="mt-6 border-b border-gray-200 pb-10">
          <p class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.18em]" style={`color: ${siteConfig.accentColor}`}>
            Blog
          </p>
          <h1 class="mt-4 text-4xl font-bold leading-tight text-gray-900 sm:text-5xl">
            Building FMAP: an interactive astro-ph paper atlas with embeddings, UMAP, and arXiv metadata
          </h1>
          <p class="mt-6 max-w-4xl text-lg leading-8 text-gray-700">
            FindMyArxivPaper (FMAP) started as a simple scientific-paper exploration pipeline and grew into a more coherent astrophysics map:
            a project that fetches astro-ph metadata from arXiv, learns useful text representations from titles and abstracts,
            evaluates both classification and retrieval, and renders the resulting paper landscape as an interactive browser-based map.
          </p>
          <div class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap gap-3 text-sm text-gray-600">
            <span class="rounded-full bg-gray-100 px-3 py-1.5">Python</span>
            <span class="rounded-full bg-gray-100 px-3 py-1.5">Sentence Transformers</span>
            <span class="rounded-full bg-gray-100 px-3 py-1.5">UMAP</span>
            <span class="rounded-full bg-gray-100 px-3 py-1.5">LinearSVC</span>
            <span class="rounded-full bg-gray-100 px-3 py-1.5">Astrophysics</span>
            <span class="rounded-full bg-gray-100 px-3 py-1.5">Interactive Visualization</span>
          </div>
          <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
            <a href="/fmap/index.html" class="inline-flex items-center rounded-full bg-gray-900 px-5 py-3 text-sm font-medium text-white hover:bg-black">
              Open 10k interactive atlas
            </a>
            <a href="https://github.com/mariadjuric/FindMyArxivPaper" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="inline-flex items-center rounded-full border border-gray-300 px-5 py-3 text-sm font-medium text-gray-800 hover:border-gray-900 hover:text-black">
              View GitHub repo / full project
            </a>
          </div>
        </header>

        <section class="mt-12">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900">Interactive map</h2>
          <p class="mt-4 text-lg leading-8 text-gray-700">
            The map below is the main output of the project. Each point is a paper, coloured by astro-ph category. The positions are not hand-made:
            they come from embedding each paper using <code>title + abstract</code>, then projecting those vectors into 2D with UMAP so that local neighborhoods stay visually meaningful.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-7 text-gray-500">
            The embedded website version is intentionally capped to a lighter public snapshot (10k points) so it stays fast and small enough for GitHub Pages. The full project and larger local map runs live in the FMAP repository.
          </p>
          <div class="mt-8 overflow-visible bg-transparent -mx-2 sm:-mx-4 lg:-mx-6 xl:-mx-8">
            <iframe
              src="/fmap/index.html"
              title="FMAP interactive atlas"
              class="h-[620px] w-full bg-black lg:h-[700px]"
              loading="lazy"
            ></iframe>
          </div>
          <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-7 text-gray-500">
            View the public interactive snapshot here: <a href="/fmap/index.html" class="underline">/fmap/index.html</a>. For the full project and larger map outputs, use the external repository link above.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="mt-16 grid gap-8 lg:grid-cols-[1.1fr,0.9fr]">
          <div>
            <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900">What the model is doing</h2>
            <p class="mt-4 text-lg leading-8 text-gray-700">
              FMAP deliberately uses <strong>two representations</strong> of the same paper dataset, because the project needs two kinds of behaviour.
            </p>
            <div class="mt-6 space-y-6">
              <div class="p-6">
                <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-gray-900">1. Category classification</h3>
                <p class="mt-3 leading-7 text-gray-700">
                  For supervised category prediction, FMAP uses <strong>TF-IDF with unigram and bigram features</strong> over the concatenated title and abstract,
                  followed by a <strong>LinearSVC</strong>. This is fast, interpretable, and surprisingly strong for scientific text, where categories often carry stable terminology.
                </p>
              </div>
              <div class="p-6">
                <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-gray-900">2. Retrieval and map structure</h3>
                <p class="mt-3 leading-7 text-gray-700">
                  Separately, FMAP embeds every paper using <strong>sentence-transformers/all-MiniLM-L6-v2</strong>. Those dense vectors are used for semantic search,
                  nearest-neighbor recommendations, retrieval metrics, and the map geometry itself.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <aside class="p-6">
            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-gray-900">Training setup</h3>
            <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-gray-700">
              <li><strong>Input text:</strong> title + abstract</li>
              <li><strong>Classifier:</strong> TF-IDF + LinearSVC</li>
              <li><strong>Embeddings:</strong> all-MiniLM-L6-v2</li>
              <li><strong>Split:</strong> 75% train / 25% test</li>
              <li><strong>Map projection:</strong> UMAP (cosine metric)</li>
              <li><strong>Retrieval metric:</strong> Precision@5</li>
            </ul>
            <p class="mt-6 text-sm leading-7 text-gray-500">
              The map uses <em>all loaded papers</em>, not just the test split. So increasing the corpus size directly makes the atlas denser.
            </p>
          </aside>
        </section>

        <section class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900">Why UMAP instead of PCA?</h2>
          <p class="mt-4 text-lg leading-8 text-gray-700">
            Earlier versions used PCA for the 2D map. PCA is easy and quick, but it often spreads semantic clusters too evenly and flattens the interesting local geometry.
            For this work, if nearby points do not feel meaningfully related, the map becomes decorative rather than useful.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-4 text-lg leading-8 text-gray-700">
            UMAP gives a much better compromise for this kind of visualization. It preserves local neighborhood structure more faithfully, makes clusters feel more natural,
            and gives the map a denser, more legible shape. In FMAP, the current map uses a moderate neighborhood size with a low minimum distance to keep the local structure compact without turning it into noise.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900">Main results and figures</h2>
          <p class="mt-4 text-lg leading-8 text-gray-700">
            FMAP writes out a small set of useful diagnostics after each run: category balance, year distribution, a 2D embedding view, and a confusion matrix for the supervised classifier.
            Together they tell you not just whether the model performs reasonably, but also what kind of corpus the map actually represents.
          </p>

          <div class="mt-10 grid gap-10">
            <div class="grid gap-10 lg:grid-cols-2">
              <figure class="overflow-visible bg-transparent">
                <img src="/images/blog/fmap-confusion-matrix.png" alt="FMAP classifier confusion matrix" class="w-full" />
                <figcaption class="px-6 py-5 text-sm leading-7 text-gray-600">
                  <strong>Confusion matrix.</strong> The classifier is not perfect, but the strong diagonal shows that category-specific language is learnable.
                  The remaining confusions are mostly between nearby astrophysical subfields rather than random failure.
                </figcaption>
              </figure>

              <figure class="overflow-visible bg-transparent">
                <img src="/images/blog/fmap-label-distribution.png" alt="FMAP category distribution" class="w-full" />
                <figcaption class="px-6 py-5 text-sm leading-7 text-gray-600">
                  <strong>Category distribution.</strong> This figure makes it obvious whether the atlas is balanced or dominated by a few astro-ph labels.
                </figcaption>
              </figure>
            </div>

            <figure class="overflow-visible bg-transparent">
              <img src="/images/blog/fmap-year-distribution.png" alt="FMAP published year distribution" class="w-full" />
              <figcaption class="px-6 py-5 text-sm leading-7 text-gray-600">
                <strong>Published year distribution.</strong> This was especially useful while debugging arXiv ingestion. It revealed when a nominally large query was still only pulling a recent slice instead of a true historical range.
              </figcaption>
            </figure>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900">How the pipeline works end-to-end</h2>
          <ol class="mt-6 list-decimal space-y-4 pl-6 text-lg leading-8 text-gray-700">
            <li>Query arXiv for astro-ph categories, optionally year-by-year over a historical range.</li>
            <li>Parse metadata and deduplicate records by arXiv URL.</li>
            <li>Build <code>combined_text = title + abstract</code>.</li>
            <li>Split the dataset into training and test partitions.</li>
            <li>Train a TF-IDF + LinearSVC classifier for category prediction.</li>
            <li>Embed every paper using a sentence-transformer.</li>
            <li>Evaluate retrieval using nearest neighbors in embedding space.</li>
            <li>Project embeddings to 2D with UMAP and generate the interactive HTML atlas.</li>
          </ol>
        </section>

      </article>
    </main>
  </body>
</html>
